        'en' => [
            'title'   => 'Laser engraver safety: eyes, fumes and fire at home and at work',
            'slug'    => 'laser-engraver-safety-guide',
            'excerpt' => 'Laser engraver safety at home, in a workshop or at school: how to protect your eyes, deal with fumes, avoid dangerous materials and prevent fires.',
            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'Is a laser engraver safe? Yes, as long as you control three hazards: laser light that can damage your eyes, smoke from the material you are working on, and the risk of fire. An enclosed Class 1 laser with fume extraction, used under supervision, works well at home, in a shop or at a school. An open-frame Class 4 machine calls for safety glasses, a separate room and far more discipline.' ],
                [ 'p', 'This is general guidance: your machine’s manual and local regulations, such as workplace or school health and safety rules, always take precedence.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Class 1 or Class 4? What the laser class means' ],
                [ 'p', 'The IEC 60825-1 standard (EN 60825-1 in Europe) sorts lasers by hazard level into Classes 1, 1M, 2, 2M, 3R, 3B and 4. The upper limit of Class 3B is 0.5 W of continuous power, so any open laser engraver rated at several or several dozen watts is Class 4: dangerous to the eyes and skin even through reflections, and able to set material alight.' ],
                [ 'p', 'Class 1 does not mean a weak laser. A high-power source may be working inside, but the enclosure with its filtering window and lid sensors keeps the radiation in. With the lid closed you can watch the job without glasses – provided the sensors work and the window is intact. Open the enclosure for servicing and you are back to Class 4 conditions.' ],
                [ 'p', 'xTool describes the enclosed S1 diode laser and the X1 platform as Class 1 machines. The S1 has five-direction flame detection, an emergency stop, a lid-open safety stop and a laser key lock; the X1 has multi-layer protection and fire safety designed for long runs.' ],
                [ 'products', '8169201631445,15473430692053' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Laser safety glasses: wavelength and optical density' ],
                [ 'p', 'Laser safety glasses only protect against a specific range of wavelengths. The frame should state the wavelengths and the optical density (OD) for each: OD 4 reduces the radiation by a factor of 10,000 and OD 7 by a factor of 10 million. In the EU, look for marking to EN 207, which gives the wavelength range and the protection level (LB).' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Diode laser (around 455 nm): visible blue light that the eye focuses onto the retina; glasses for it usually have orange or red lenses.',
                    'Fibre and MOPA lasers and IR modules (around 1064 nm): invisible infrared that also reaches the retina, and the blink reflex does not react to it.',
                    'CO₂ laser (10.6 µm): absorbed by the cornea and skin, but also by polycarbonate, which is why CO₂ glasses can be clear – and give no protection against a diode laser.',
                    'UV laser (typically 355 nm): invisible radiation that mainly harms the cornea and the lens of the eye.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'An example: xTool’s goggles for the MetalFab welder are rated OD7+ for 1030–1100 nm, so they do not guarantee protection against a 455 nm diode laser. With interchangeable modules – the S1 with its 1064 nm module, or the X1, which supports five laser types (CO₂, diode, UV, MOPA and fibre) – choose glasses for each module.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Why sunglasses are not protection' ],
                [ 'p', 'Sunglasses, even with a UV400 filter, are designed for sunlight, not for a focused laser beam. Dark lenses that do not block the wavelength can even make things worse, because your pupils open wider. Treat cheap goggles without markings the same way.' ],
                [ 'p', 'In enclosed machines, the window in the enclosure does the job of the glasses. According to xTool, the F1 Ultra with a 20 W fibre laser and a diode laser has an enclosed cover, an eye-protection cover and a fire safety stop; xTool lists the same features for the F2 Ultra. Glasses are mainly needed for work with the enclosure open – the manual tells you when.' ],
                [ 'products', '8523126931669' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Fumes and smoke: ducting outside or an air purifier?' ],
                [ 'p', 'A laser vaporises and burns material, producing smoke full of fine particles and volatile organic compounds – and, with plywood and MDF, breakdown products of the glue. The finest particles are invisible. Smoke also settles on the optics and reduces laser power.' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Ducting outside through a window or a ventilation duct is the most effective option. Keep the hose short and airtight; an inline duct fan such as the xTool SafetyPro™ IF2 boosts the airflow and can switch on together with the laser.',
                    'An air purifier with a pre-filter, HEPA and activated carbon helps when you cannot duct outside. The xTool SafetyPro™ AP2 (150 m³/h) is designed for desktop machines and the AP2 Max (700 m³/h) for large laser cutters; the filters need regular replacement.',
                    'After a job, wait until the extraction has cleared the smoke before you open the lid.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'More power means more smoke. The xTool P3 is an 80 W CO₂ laser with a 914 × 457 mm bed for batch cutting of plywood or acrylic, so plan the extraction before you buy. According to xTool, the P3’s four cameras are used not only to position the material but also for safety monitoring.' ],
                [ 'products', '9089720189141' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Materials you must never laser cut' ],
                [ 'p', 'Rule number one: if you don’t know what a material is made of, don’t put it under the laser.' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'PVC and vinyl – never: sheets, pipes, vinyl film and PVC faux leather. They release hydrogen chloride, which irritates the airways and corrodes the machine. PVC carries recycling code 3.',
                    'Other plastics containing chlorine or fluorine, such as PTFE (Teflon) – for the same reason.',
                    'Polycarbonate – don’t cut it. It chars, yellows and catches fire easily, and it looks just like acrylic.',
                    'ABS – only with care: it melts, smokes heavily, and the smoke can contain hydrogen cyanide. At most a light engraving with strong extraction, if the manual allows it.',
                    'Glass-fibre and carbon-fibre composites – harmful resin fumes and fine dust.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'Wood, laser plywood, acrylic (PMMA), paper, cardboard and natural leather are safer, but they also smoke and need extraction.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'How to prevent a laser fire' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Never leave a running machine unattended, even with flame sensors – they are the last line of defence, not a babysitter.',
                    'Use air assist when cutting: it blows the flame away from the cut and protects the lens.',
                    'Test your settings on a scrap piece. Moving too slowly at high power on wood or cardboard easily ends in flames.',
                    'Clean the honeycomb bed and the crumb tray – offcuts and resin residue are ready-made fuel.',
                    'Keep an extinguisher suitable for electrical equipment within reach, for example a CO₂ extinguisher, which leaves no powder in the optics, plus a fire blanket.',
